<ontology/use_brewing_distilling/use_brewing_distilling__0056__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 208" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n208> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Hops used in Brewing, Malting & Distilling: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 208"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "HOP: a climbing plant, the Humulus lupulus, cultivated for its bitter principle, which forms an important element in brewing; the fine hops being used for pale ales, the common, or less fine, for porter. There were, in 1855, nearly 38,000 acres of hop gardens under cultivation in England, which produced about 83,250,000 lbs. of hops. Hop-vines, abounding in ..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_brewing_distilling/use_brewing_distilling__0056__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. BEER" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Hops used in Brewing, Malting & Distilling: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. BEER"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "After the end of a year or two, hops are reckoned to have lost much of their marketable value, and are then sold to the second-rate porter brewers, under the name of old hops."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

