<ontology/use_calico_printing/use_calico_printing__0536__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 340" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n340> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Wood and Timber Dye Woods Brazil Wood used in Calico Cloth & Calico Printing: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 340"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "SAPAN-WOOD: a dye-wood obtained in Malabar, and the islands of the Eastern seas, from the Cesalpinia sappan, C. coriaria, and pulcherrima. A decoction of the wood is used by calico-printers for red dyes."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_calico_printing/use_calico_printing__0536__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. C D E F" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Wood and Timber Dye Woods Brazil Wood used in Calico Cloth & Calico Printing: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. C D E F"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "They are next rinsed in cold water, and steeped in water very faintly acidulated with sulphuric acid, during 4 hours, then rinsed, and dried. Preparation of Mordants.--1 gallon of boiling water; 2 pounds of alum; dissolve: 1 pound of acetate of lead; 4 ounces of sal-ammoniac; 1 of chalk; mix well together; after decomposition and subsidence, draw off clear. ..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "essay_context" .

