<ontology/use_chocolate_confectionery/use_chocolate_confectionery__doc_vanilla__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 409" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n409> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Vanilla used in Chocolate & Confectionery: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 409"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "VANILLA: an exquisite perfume and aromatic, the thin pod-like capsule of the Vanilla planifolia, a native of South America. Vanilla is one of the most esteemed of all aromatics, and very expensive, costing five or six guineas the pound. It is used to flavour chocolate and confectioneries, and is much esteemed on the Continent as an ingredient in some stimula..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_chocolate_confectionery/use_chocolate_confectionery__doc_vanilla__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 976" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n976> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Vanilla used in Chocolate & Confectionery: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 976"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "VANILLA: (GER. Vanilje. DU. Ba- nille. DA. Vaniller. SW. Baniller. FR. Vanille. IR. Vainiglia. SP. Vainilla, Vainica. PORT. Vainilha, Baonelha. LAT. Vanilla.) There are 124 species of the vanilla plant, one of which produces this fruit, which is used in making chocolate. It is a native of Mexico, and also of some parts of the East Indies. It is a parasitic p..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_chocolate_confectionery/use_chocolate_confectionery__doc_vanilla__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section VANILLA" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15191> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Vanilla used in Chocolate & Confectionery: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section VANILLA"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "VANILLA: The fleshy, pod-like, odoriferous fruit of different species of Epidendrum constitute the substance called vanilla, which is used in confectionery for giving a delicious perfume to chocolate, liqueurs, &c. As an aromatic it is much sought after by confectioners, for flavoring ices and creams; and also by perfumers, liqueurists, and distillers. The b..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

