<ontology/use_cordage_shipbuilding/use_cordage_shipbuilding__0207__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. F, F" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Tar used in Shipbuilding, Rope, Cordage & Sailcloth: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. F, F"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Chapman of Newcastle, to whom the art of rope-making is deeply indebted, having observed that rope yarn is considerably weakened by passing through the tar-kettle, that tarred cordage loses its strength progressively in cold climates, and so rapidly in hot climates as to be scarcely fit for use in three years, discovered that the deterioration was due to the..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_cordage_shipbuilding/use_cordage_shipbuilding__0207__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 874" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n874/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Tar used in Shipbuilding, Rope, Cordage & Sailcloth: Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 874"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "We have hitherto confined our observations only to the manufacture of ropes themselves; we must now say a few words on the adventitious aid sought from tar, and other things, in order to augment the strength and durability of cordage."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

