<ontology/use_glass_ceramics/use_glass_ceramics__1895__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 547" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n547> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Kelp used in Glassmaking, Pottery & Porcelain: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 547"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "KELP: in the glass trade, a term used for a sort of potash made use of in many of the glass-works, particularly for the green glass. It is the calcined ashes of a plant called by the same name; and in some places \"sea-tags,\" or \"laces,\" a sort of thick-leaved fucus or sea-wrack. This plant is thrown on the rocks and shores in great quantities, and in the sum..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_glass_ceramics/use_glass_ceramics__1895__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 886" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n886> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Kelp used in Glassmaking, Pottery & Porcelain: J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 886"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "KELP: A substance composed of different materials, of which the fossil or mineral alkali or, as it is commonly termed, soda, is the chief. This ingredient renders it useful in the composition of soap, in the manufacture of alum, and in the formation of crown and bottle glass. It is formed of marine plants, which, being cut from the rocks with a hook, are col..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_glass_ceramics/use_glass_ceramics__1895__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SPONGE" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Kelp used in Glassmaking, Pottery & Porcelain: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SPONGE"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "The glass proper for receiving these vitrifying pigments, should be colourless, uniform, and difficult of fusion; for which reason crown glass, made with little alkali, or with kelp, is preferred."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

