    rdfs:label "Evidence for Charcoal used in Gunpowder, Explosives & Lucifer Matches: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 95"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "VEGETABLE CHARCOAL: charred or burnt wood, which is largely used for fuel on the Continent, and is also valued for making glass, steel, and as a deodorizer when powdered; cylinder charcoal obtained by distilling non-resinous woods is used in the manufacture of gunpowder. See CARBON."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_gunpowder_matches/use_gunpowder_matches__1248__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. GUNPOWDER" ;
