<ontology/use_leather_tanning/use_leather_tanning__0180__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 999" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n999> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Myrabolans used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 999"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "MYROBALANS: The dried fruits of different varieties of terminalia. The fruit, varying from the size of an olive to that of a gall-nut, consists of a white pentangular nut, covered by a substance about 2 lines in thickness. The latter, which is the only valuable part, is mucilaginous and highly astringent; and being separated from the nut is employed, with th..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_leather_tanning/use_leather_tanning__0180__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section MYROBALANS" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15191> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Myrabolans used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section MYROBALANS"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "MYROBALANS: This is a name applied to the almond-like kernels of a nut or dried fruit of the plum kind, of which there are several sorts known in the East. They are the produce of various species of Terminalia, as T. Bellerica, chebula, citrina, and angustifolia. They vary from the size of olives to that of gall nuts, and have a rough, bitter, and unpleasant..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_leather_tanning/use_leather_tanning__0180__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Watt (1908), Commercial Products of India, scan p. 1086" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b31361584/page/n1086/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Myrabolans used in Leather Tanning, Tawing & Currying: Watt (1908), Commercial Products of India, scan p. 1086"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Trade Names. Dye and Tan.—The dried fruit of this species constitutes the “Chebulic” and “Black” myrobalan of commerce, one of the most valuable of Indian tanning materials."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

