    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "This is regularly collected and, like honey, enters more largely than sugar into the pharmaceutical preparations of the Hindus (see Bamboo, p. 111, and Honey, pp. 128-9). [Cf."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0097__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 24" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n24/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Honey used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 24"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "It grows like oats, but in quality resembles rice. ÆGYPTIACUM, a name formerly given to several medicinal preparations, the only one of which now in use is that called mel ægyptiacum, which is composed of honey and verdigris."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

