<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0683__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 348" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n348> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Senna used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 348"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "SENNA: the leaflets of several species of Cassia, used in medicine for their purgative properties. C. elongata and C. acutifolia, furnish the Timnevelly and Alexandrian senna, which are the best. The latter constitutes the bulk of the imports into Europe. It is much adulterated with the leaves of Cynanchum Argel, Tephrosia Apollinea, and Coriaria myrtifolia...."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0683__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1352" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n1352> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Senna used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1352"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "SENNA: (Fr. séné; Ger. sennablätter; Ital. senna; Span. sen; Lat. cassia senna; Arab. suna). The plant (cassia senna) which yields the leaves known in commerce and the materia medica by the name of senna, is an annual, growing wild in the interior of Africa, and is more especially a native of Upper Egypt. The leaves are collected by the Arabs frequenting the..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0683__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section SENNA" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15191> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Senna used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section SENNA"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Alexandrian senna, according to Mr. Jacob Bell (\"Pharmaceutical Journal,\" vol. 2, p. 63), contains a mixture of two or more species of true senna."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

