<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0697__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. TINCTURE" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Spirits used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. TINCTURE"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "TINCTURE is a title used by apothecaries to designate alcohol, in a somewhat dilute state, impregnated with the active principles of either vegetable or animal substances."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0697__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 864" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n864/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Spirits used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 864"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Tinctures drawn from both with rectified spirit have nearly the same taste: on distilling off the menstruum, the extract left from the tincture of the East India rhubarb proved considerably the strongest."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__0697__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Watt (1908), Commercial Products of India, scan p. 916" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b31361584/page/n916/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Spirits used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: Watt (1908), Commercial Products of India, scan p. 916"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "The process adopted was that recommended in the British Pharmacopœia, which consists in exhausting the drug by percolation with alcohol, concentrating the percolate and precipitating the resin by adding water."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

