<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__1701__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 321" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n321> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Lignum Quassia used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 321"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "QUASSIA: a name for some bitter woods—the Surinam quassia is the produce of Quassia amara, and the Jamaican quassia of Picranea excelsa. They furnish a piro and simple bitter, and brewers are said to use the chips freely as a substitute for hops, although they have narcotic properties."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__1701__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1266" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n1266> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Lignum Quassia used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 1266"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "QUASSIA: (Ger. quassienholz; Fr. bois de quassie; Span. leño de casia). A beautiful tall tree (Quassia amara), growing in North and South America, and the West Indies. The wood is dense, tough, of a pale yellow colour, and odoriferous. Taste intensely and purely bitter. (British Pharmacopœia, 1867.) It is pretty extensively employed in the materia medica; an..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_pharmaceuticals/use_pharmaceuticals__1701__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. QUASSIA" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Lignum Quassia used in Pharmaceuticals & Druggists' Preparations: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. QUASSIA"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "QUASSIA: is the wood of the root of the Quassia excelsa, a tree which grows in Surinam, the East Indies, &c. It affords to water an intensely bitter decoction, which is occasionally used in medicine, and was formerly substituted by some brewers for hops, but is now prohibited under severe penalties. It affords a safe and efficacious fly-water, or poison for ..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

