<ontology/use_rubber_waterproofing/use_rubber_waterproofing__1504__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. CAOUTCHOUC, GUM-ELASTIC, OR INDIAN-RUBBER" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Oil Seed used in India-Rubber, Gutta-Percha & Waterproofing: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. CAOUTCHOUC, GUM-ELASTIC, OR INDIAN-RUBBER"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Caoutchouc dissolves in the fixed oils, such as linseed oil, but the varnish has not the property of becoming concrete upon exposure to air."@en ;
