<ontology/use_soap_manufacture/use_soap_manufacture__0562__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 111" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n111> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Rosin used in Soap Manufacture: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 111"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "COLOPHONY: a name for the ordinary rosin or resin of commerce, being the residuum remaining in the body of the still after common turpentine has been submitted to distillation, for the manufacture of the oil of turpentine. The black colophony is the cooled brittle mass, in the state in which it leaves the still; the amber or yellow-coloured, is the same resi..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_soap_manufacture/use_soap_manufacture__0562__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SOAP" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Rosin used in Soap Manufacture: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SOAP"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Rosin, although very soluble in alkaline menstrua, is not however susceptible, like fats, of being transformed into an acid, and will not of course saponify, or form a proper soap by itself."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_soap_manufacture/use_soap_manufacture__0562__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 936" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n936/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Rosin used in Soap Manufacture: Mortimer (1823), General Commercial Dictionary, scan p. 936"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Soap, made with tallow and soda, has a white colour, and therefore known by the name of white soap; but it is usual for soap-makers, in order to lower the price of the article, to mix a considerable portion of rosin with the tallow; this mixture forms the common yellow or turpentine soap."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

