<ontology/use_varnish_paint_lacquer/use_varnish_paint_lacquer__0860__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 117" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/b21780535/page/n117> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Gum Copal used in Varnish, Paint & Lacquer: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Dictionary of Trade Products (1892), scan p. 117"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "COPAL: an important pure resin of commerce, forming the basis of most varnishes; a general name for most clear resins."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_varnish_paint_lacquer/use_varnish_paint_lacquer__0860__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 249" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/generalcommercia00mort/page/n249> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Gum Copal used in Varnish, Paint & Lacquer: Thomas Mortimer (rev. Dickinson), A General Commercial Dictionary (1823), scan p. 249"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "COPAL: a substance of great importance as a varnish, obtained from the rhus copalinum, a tree in North America. It is a beautiful transparent resinous-like substance. When heated it melts like other resins; but it differs from them in not being soluble in alcohol, or in oil of turpentine, without peculiar management. Neither does it dissolve in the fixed oil..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_varnish_paint_lacquer/use_varnish_paint_lacquer__0860__q3/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. VARNISH" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Gum Copal used in Varnish, Paint & Lacquer: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. VARNISH"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Indeed, the whitest copal varnish can be made only in this way; for if the drying oil have been heated to nearly its boiling point, it becomes coloured, and darkens the varnish."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

