<ontology/use_woollen_silk_dyeing/use_woollen_silk_dyeing__0302__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. BRONZING" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Madder used in Woollen, Silk & Worsted Dyeing: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. BRONZING"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Wool or silk may also receive a light blue ground from the indigo vat, then be mordanted with alum, washed, and turned through a madder bath till the wished-for brown be brought out."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_woollen_silk_dyeing/use_woollen_silk_dyeing__0302__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "McCulloch (1880), Dictionary of Commerce, scan p. 944" ;
    dcterms:source <https://archive.org/details/dictionaryprac00mccu/page/n944/mode/1up> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Madder used in Woollen, Silk & Worsted Dyeing: McCulloch (1880), Dictionary of Commerce, scan p. 944"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "The Dutch madder is more employed by the woollen dyers, and the French by the cotton dyers and printers. ‘In making purchases of garance, it is essential to employ a house of confidence, because the quality depends entirely upon the care and honesty of the agent."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

