<ontology/use_woollen_silk_dyeing/use_woollen_silk_dyeing__0536__q1/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section BRAZIL WOOD" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15191> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Wood and Timber Dye Woods Brazil Wood used in Woollen, Silk & Worsted Dyeing: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section BRAZIL WOOD"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "BRAZIL WOOD: This very ponderous wood is obtained in Brazil from the Caesalpina Braziliensis, which yields a red or crimson dye, when united with alum or tartar, and is used by silk dyers. It is imported principally from Pernambuco, 1,200 quintals having been shipped to London in 1835, but about 500 tons, worth about L4 a ton, were imported from Costa Rica i..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

<ontology/use_woollen_silk_dyeing/use_woollen_silk_dyeing__0536__q2/#resource> dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SANDAL" ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/44562> ;
    a <ontology/EvidenceQuote/#resource> ;
    rdfs:label "Evidence for Wood and Timber Dye Woods Brazil Wood used in Woollen, Silk & Worsted Dyeing: Andrew Ure, A Dictionary of Arts, Manufactures, and Mines (2nd ed., 1840; Project Gutenberg #44562), s.v. SANDAL"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "Sandal wood is used in India, along with one-tenth of sapan wood (the Cæsalpinia sapan of Japan, Java, Siam, Celebes, and the Philippine isles), principally for dyeing silk and cotton."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .

